<p>A Paris criminal court sentenced Swiss Islamic scholar Tariq Ramadan to 18 years in prison on Wednesday after finding him guilty of raping three women in France. Judges also issued an arrest warrant after Ramadan did not appear in person for the verdict. Ramadan has consistently denied the accusations and says he is innocent.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/I7F5IYCN-AN5bUI7sBGM96PBVI50X69qyg5NwWsfhN_kdUdPBpSA6Tk3K6kQsVQVzbCZ5U0bomk6E-PhxsP0GkmGYa3SU9iZmJ3TCyYevOHhLIMpR1_ZLDqybuMwJWhAkpzOn6A6zbBTNQB0uRlb7e2EQ7_AGUfSEeP7yNyl_93zA0Wi' alt='Swiss Islamic scholar Tariq Ramadan listens to his lawyers during a press conference in Paris on September 30, 2025.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Case profile: Deutsche Welle described the proceedings as one of the most high-profile French cases linked to the #MeToo movement.</li><li>Public image: SCMP said Ramadan was a charismatic but controversial figure in European Islam and had taught at Oxford University.</li><li>Cross-border cases: SCMP reported that Ramadan faces a series of rape allegations in Switzerland and France and had already been convicted in a separate Swiss case.</li><li>Trial timing: Ramadan had been on trial in Paris since March 2 before the court delivered its verdict on March 26.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Ramadan&#x27;s lawyer: His lawyer said Ramadan missed court because he was hospitalized with complications linked to multiple sclerosis, an explanation later rejected by a court-ordered medical assessment. (<a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/police-and-justice/article/2026/03/26/french-court-finds-swiss-islamic-scholar-ramadan-guilty-of-rape-sentenced-to-18-years_6751822_105.html'>Le Monde</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/europe/article/3347919/french-court-finds-swiss-islamic-scholar-ramadan-guilty-rape'>French court sentences Islamic scholar to 18 years in prison for rape</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-ne